<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Human Freedom" cic="CCC 1730-1748" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in groups. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Recalling the essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What is the Catechism's definition of freedom in CCC 1731? Learn it by heart: &laquo;Freedom is the power...&raquo;</li>
<li>What two modern errors about freedom does the lesson identify? Explain them in your own words.</li>
<li>Why does the story of Pinocchio illustrate the Christian view of freedom? At what moment does Pinocchio become a &laquo;real boy&raquo;?</li>
<li>What does it mean that grace &laquo;does not destroy freedom but perfects it&raquo; (CCC 1742)?</li>
<li>Name at least four factors that, according to the Catechism, can diminish the imputability of an action (CCC 1735).</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For reflection and writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question sincerely. There are no &laquo;correct&raquo; answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. What three things make you more free? And what three things bind you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Make two lists:</p>
<p class="question-block__p"><strong>Make me free:</strong></p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="margin-top:4px">___________ ___________ ___________</p>
<p class="question-block__p"><strong>Bind me:</strong></p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="margin-top:4px">___________ ___________ ___________</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Think about Jesus' words: &laquo;everyone who commits sin is a slave to sin&raquo; (Jn 8:34). Is there a habitual sin in your life from which you would like to be freed?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Write it here (for yourself only). Then write one concrete step you could take this week to begin the path toward freedom.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. St. Maximilian Kolbe gave his life for a stranger in Auschwitz. What made him free in that moment: what he did, or what he stopped doing?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Think of someone in your life who needs help. Is there something you could freely do for that person that would cost you? What holds you back?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. Solzhenitsyn found inner freedom in the Gulag, the Soviet prison. Do you believe inner freedom is possible in very difficult external situations? Have you experienced something like this?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Describe a situation where, even though circumstances were against you, you felt free on the inside.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. St. Paul writes: &laquo;Do not use your freedom as an opportunity for the flesh, but through love serve one another&raquo; (Gal 5:13). In what areas of your life do you use freedom as an excuse to do what you want rather than to serve?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be specific. Christian freedom is not dressing selfishness up in fine-sounding talk.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical activities</h3>

<h4>A. The Two Cages</h4>
<p>Draw two cages on a sheet of paper. In the first cage, write or draw what oppresses or binds you from the outside: social pressures, fears, circumstances. In the second cage, write or draw what enslaves you from the inside: sins, attachments, resentments. Then write at the bottom of the page: &laquo;For freedom Christ has set us free&raquo; (Gal 5:1). Pray for a few moments in silence, asking Christ to open those cages.</p>

<h4>B. The Paradox of Freedom</h4>
<p>The lesson says that Christian freedom is free to serve. The world says freedom means not having to serve anyone. Write a brief dialogue between a young Christian and a friend who thinks faith is a prison. The Christian explains that faith has actually made him free. (8-10 lines)</p>
